# Cachy environment configuration — copy to `.env` and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored. Never commit real secrets.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Shared secret between the browser and this server's API routes.
#
# Guards 17 routes, including /api/orders, /api/tpsl, /api/positions,
# /api/account, /api/balance, every /api/sync/* endpoint and the three AI
# proxies (/api/ai/anthropic, /api/ai/gemini, /api/ai/openai).
#
# Authentication FAILS CLOSED: if this is unset, all of those routes answer 401
# and the app cannot talk to its own backend. That is deliberate — an unset
# secret is a misconfiguration, not a permission grant. Without it a public
# deployment would be an open relay, with AI calls billed to the operator.
# See docs/adr/0002-api-authentication-fails-closed.md.
#
# The same value goes into the app's Settings → Connections → App Access Token,
# so the browser can send it as the `x-app-access-token` header.
#
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
APP_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Enables the /api/stream-logs endpoint. Leave unset to keep log streaming off;
# the endpoint denies access in production when this is missing.
# LOG_STREAM_KEY=

# Node environment. Set to `production` for deployed instances.
# NODE_ENV=development

# Default Ollama base URL for the AI proxies (/api/ai/ollama and
# /api/ai/ollama/models) when a request carries no baseUrl of its own.
#
# Historically the proxies defaulted to http://localhost:11434. Since the SSRF
# fix (BUG-0291) the server refuses loopback/reserved targets, so that default
# became a silent 403 for hosted Cachy + local Ollama setups (BUG-0295). Point
# this at an Ollama instance reachable FROM THE SERVER instead — a LAN IP or
# hostname (e.g. http://192.168.1.10:11434), never localhost.
#
# The value passes through the same reserved-IP filter as user-supplied URLs:
# loopback/private targets stay rejected even here. Leave unset to require an
# explicit baseUrl from clients (they then get a 400 explaining exactly this).
# OLLAMA_PROXY_BASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RUNTIME (the built server, @sveltejs/adapter-node)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Port the built server listens on. server.js defaults it to 3001 rather than
# adapter-node's own 3000, because port 3000 is often taken on shared hosting.
# deploy.sh sets it per environment from STABLE_PORT / BETA_PORT in .deploy.conf.
# PORT=3001

# Public origin of the deployment, e.g. https://cachy.app. Behind a reverse
# proxy the server cannot infer this, and SvelteKit needs it to resolve
# `event.url` and to pass its cross-origin check on form submissions.
# Not read by application code — adapter-node reads it. See DEPLOYMENT.md.
# ORIGIN=

# Header adapter-node reads the real client IP from when this app sits behind
# a reverse proxy (this app's own documented deployment shape — aaPanel's
# nginx, see DEPLOYMENT.md). Without this, `event.getClientAddress()` returns
# the proxy's own address for every request, so the per-IP rate limits in
# src/lib/server/clientToken.ts and /api/auth/token (BUG-0052) bucket every
# visitor together instead of separating them.
#
# ONLY set this if the app is not directly reachable from the internet on its
# own PORT — otherwise a caller can forge this header and spoof any IP,
# bypassing every per-IP rate limit outright.
# Not read by application code — adapter-node reads it.
# ADDRESS_HEADER=X-Forwarded-For

# How many proxy hops to trust when reading ADDRESS_HEADER, counting from the
# right of a comma-separated list. 1 for a single reverse proxy (aaPanel's
# nginx sitting directly in front of this app) — set higher only if another
# trusted proxy sits in front of that one.
# Not read by application code — adapter-node reads it.
# XFF_DEPTH=1

# adapter-node honours further variables (HOST, BODY_SIZE_LIMIT,
# SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT, ...) that this app never reads itself. They are documented
# upstream: https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/adapter-node#Environment-variables

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT SET HERE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Deployment tooling is configured in `.deploy.conf` (see .deploy.conf.example),
# not in this file — including DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL, which scripts/discord-notify.sh
# reads from the shell environment.
#
# `src/tests/env_documentation.test.ts` fails if application code reads a
# variable that is not listed above. Keep them in one place.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A NOTE ON VITE_-PREFIXED VARIABLES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Vite inlines every VITE_-prefixed variable into the client bundle at build
# time, so anything named that way is public: it is served as plain JavaScript to
# every visitor. Never put a secret behind a VITE_ name.
#
# The AI key settings once defaulted to VITE_OPENAI_API_KEY / VITE_GEMINI_API_KEY
# / VITE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY. That fallback is gone (roadmap item 24a) — setting
# those variables now has no effect. Each user enters their own key in
# Settings → AI; those keys are Class A data under ADR-0001 and stay in the
# browser's localStorage.
#
# Only VITE_APP_VERSION is set today, by vite.config.ts from package.json.
